## Safe area (notch / cutout) — required in BOTH orientations

- Manifest/window: `LAYOUT_IN_DISPLAY_CUTOUT_MODE_SHORT_EDGES`.
- Apply padding via `setOnApplyWindowInsetsListener`:
  - **portrait** → top inset (status bar + top notch),
  - **landscape** → left + right insets (side cameras).
- Re-apply on `onConfigurationChanged` and after lock/unlock.

The CSS injection in the page is a **separate** mechanism with a much narrower
job than it looks. The native padding above already handles the cutout; all the
CSS has to do is stop the page adding a *second* inset of its own through
`env(safe-area-inset-*)`. So it zeroes the safe-area custom properties on
`:root` — and touches nothing else.

**The constraint is on properties, not on selectors.** `padding-left`,
`padding-right` and `margin` must never be written, on any selector: those are
what sites build their gutters with, and zeroing them on `html`/`body`/`#app`
flattens the whole layout against both screen edges. `padding-top` is allowed
only on chrome wrappers that add a status-bar offset of their own
(`.app-header`, `.gameview-mobile-header`). Read pitfalls #10 before editing
this — the earlier "fix" for it named the wrong culprit and shipped the bug for
two generations.

