## Keyboard must not cover inputs

**Read `kotlin_keyboard.mdc` and implement what it says.** Do not improvise
here — `adjustResize` plus a JS `scrollIntoView` looks correct in a five-minute
test and jitters visibly in real use, because the engine and the injection then
scroll the same page and their corrections add up.

The shape of the solution: keep `adjustResize` in the manifest so the animation
callback fires, strip the IME insets before they reach the WebView, and slide
the view with `translationY` by an amount computed from the field position the
page reports over a JS bridge, clamped to the height the keyboard settles at.

---

## Loading session and the cover

Two different mechanisms, never mixed up:

- **The loading screen (splash + bar)** belongs to the launch. One per launch,
  shown by the router, filled to 100% before anything replaces it. It must
  never reappear: not after the notification-permission screen, not for a push
  URL on a live shell, not between redirect hops.
- **The cover** belongs to the WebView, and it covers **one** thing: the
  session's first page, where all that sits underneath is an empty view. A
  scrim plus a small spinner, raised in `onPageStarted` while
  `firstPageSettled` is false, dropped a beat after `onPageFinished`.

Everything else navigates uncovered. A redirect hop, an in-page navigation, a
retry — the page the user is already reading stays on screen until the next one
commits, which is both what a browser does and what the user asked for the
first time they saw a scrim flash on every hop (pitfalls #33). Covering hops
looks like polish and reads as the app freezing.

Back the cover with a timeout so a page that never reports back cannot hold the
screen for good, and drop it on the paths that never produce a finished page —
a load that turns into a download, an unsupported scheme handed to the system,
a load that failed with no retry left. **No branch of `onReceivedError` may end
without either navigating somewhere or dropping the cover** (pitfalls #31).

**Never snapshot the WebView to build a freeze-frame.** `view.draw(canvas)` on
a hardware-accelerated WebView yields a black bitmap, which is exactly the
"black screen during redirects" the client reported. A scrim over the live view
costs nothing and cannot be black.

---

## Redirect loop (ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS)

Chromium gives up after 20 hops; affiliate chains are routinely longer, so this
is an ordinary condition to resume from, not a failure to report.

**Keep two separate URL fields.** They are not interchangeable and conflating
them is pitfalls #30:

| Field | Written by | Read by |
|---|---|---|
| `deepestHop` | `shouldOverrideUrlLoading` (main frame) **and** `onPageStarted` | the redirect retry |
| `lastMainFrameUrl` | `onPageFinished` only | renderer recovery, offline return URL |

The retry resumes from `deepestHop`. Resuming from the last *settled* page
restarts the chain into the same loop and spends the budget on nothing. Both
write sites matter: `shouldOverrideUrlLoading` does not see every server-side
30x, and `onPageStarted` gives the URL the engine actually committed to.

**Post the retry, never call `loadUrl` from inside the callback.** The engine
is still unwinding the failed navigation and defers a re-entrant load by
seconds — which then looks like a slow partner rather than a bug in the shell.
`view.postDelayed({ view.loadUrl(resumeAt) }, 60L)`.

**Give the exhausted budget somewhere to go.** `-9` is not a network error, so
falling through does nothing at all (pitfalls #31). Retry the configured entry
point once, then drop the cover and hand the page back.

Two flags make the retry work, and both were bugs before they were flags:

- `loadFailed`, set in `onReceivedError` and cleared in `onPageStarted`. A
  failed load still reaches `onPageFinished`, with the error page committed. If
  that path resets the retry counter and records the URL as settled, the budget
  never runs down and the error page is handed to the user.
- `retryPending`, set while a reload is queued, so nothing treats the gap as a
  settled page.

