## File upload (no storage permission)

`WebChromeClient.onShowFileChooser` → launch `params.createIntent()` via an
`ActivityResultContracts.StartActivityForResult` launcher, return the result
with `FileChooserParams.parseResult`. Camera/gallery work through the chooser.

---

## Back navigation

`OnBackPressedCallback`: if `webView.canGoBack()` → `goBack()`, else do nothing
(back on the first page must NOT close the app). System back + gestures both
route through this.

---

## Offline detection (see pitfalls #11)

- `NetWire.connectivityFlow` via `registerDefaultNetworkCallback`
  (`onLost`/`onUnavailable` → false) collected in the WebView screen.
- A ~4s heartbeat that checks `isConnected()` for the "page already loaded, then
  internet dropped" case.
- On offline: blank the WebView (`about:blank`) then open OfflinePortal with the
  current URL as the return target. Retry reloads it (handle in `onNewIntent`).

---

## Push URL into the WebView (one-time)

### Two delivery shapes, and only one of them reaches your service

This is the single most-missed thing in the push path (pitfalls #32). Which
code runs on a notification tap depends on the **payload**, not on your code:

| Payload | App foregrounded | App backgrounded or killed |
|---|---|---|
| `data` only | `PushRelay.onMessageReceived` | `PushRelay.onMessageReceived` |
| contains `notification` | `PushRelay.onMessageReceived` | **Firebase draws it. Your service never runs.** |

In the bottom-right cell the tap opens the plain launcher intent, and the
`data` payload arrives as raw string extras — `url`, `link` — with none of your
own extras and no `from_push` flag. A router that reads only its own extras
drops the link silently and reopens the shell on the saved page, which looks
exactly like "push URLs don't work" while foreground tests pass.

So the router resolves a push URL from **both** shapes, in `onCreate` and in
`onNewIntent`. The NATIVE lock and `UrlGuard` apply after the resolver,
unchanged. Agree the payload shape with whoever sends the pushes: a link that
lives only inside the `notification` block cannot be read at all.

### The rest of the contract

- `PushBus.onWarmUrl` is set in `StreamPortal.onStart`, cleared in
  `onStop`/`onDestroy`.
- `PushRelay.onMessageReceived`: if `PushBus.onWarmUrl != null` and the payload
  has a `url` → call it (loads into the live WebView) and DON'T show a
  notification / DON'T persist. Otherwise save `coldPushUrl` and post a
  notification.
- Cold start: `WelcomePortal` consumes `coldPushUrl` BEFORE the state machine
  and opens the WebView on it. The loading bar still fills first — a cold push
  is a launch like any other.
- Warm tap while the shell is alive: the launcher activity must not draw
  anything. No content view, no splash, no transition — hand the URL to the
  live shell over the bus, `finish()`, and `overridePendingTransition(0, 0)`.
  The user sees the page they left, then the pushed URL when it loads.
  Two details make this work: the bus needs a **queue** as well as a callback
  (the shell clears its callback in `onStop`, and a background tap arrives
  exactly then — queue it and consume it in `onStart`), and the notification's
  `PendingIntent` must **not** carry `FLAG_ACTIVITY_CLEAR_TOP`, which would
  destroy the very shell being handed to.
- Never persist a push URL beyond its one-time use.

